{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793698620,"nonce":"JyD2yX7bBHROsVMmJ2mx4xJLBTM=","title":"zylua: a local bound once to a fresh table has its constructor's class, so its method calls are single-target","message":"method_call (and Lua OOP written the usual way) pays for two-target dispatch at every call on an instance, because the shape of a Toggle.new result has classes {Toggle, NthToggle}: NthToggle.new makes its object with Toggle.new and then re-metatables it. Measured on the NUC (e8fe1d3): with every call single-target the row runs 27 ms wall against 52, 1.6 ms elapsed against 7.8, and the main chunk compiles in 10 ms against 24. In the lowered chunk the optimizer spends about 4.4 us per instruction and a two-target site is about 59 instructions against about 20 for a single-target one (leaner dispatch code alone would save about 15 percent of the chunk, about 7 percent of the row's wall; a forwarding-block cleanup before round 0 saved 2.5 percent; neither is worth it).\n\nDesign, sound and conservative. A local `t` has the exact class C when all of these hold:\n1. t is declared `local t = F(...)`, never assigned again and not captured (VarInfo.assigned, captured; and the debug-library guards non_nil_vars uses).\n2. F is a fresh constructor with class C: every return is `setmetatable({...}, C)` with C a never-nil class, or a local `o` that is single-assigned from a fresh constructor call or table constructor, is given `setmetatable(o, C)` by a top-level statement of F before the return, and is used in F only as a field read or write target, that setmetatable's first argument, and the returned value. For `NthToggle.new` the callee must be resolved with the declaration's presence known (the lowering's `known_present`), because the types alone allow `NthToggle.new` to be absent and fall through `__index` to `Toggle.new`.\n3. Every `setmetatable` that can reach a table of t's shape (the typer's metatable_builtin, which today only adds C to the shape's classes) is one of: a constructor built in the call (birth), or `setmetatable(o, X)` where o is a local with !assigned, !captured, initialised from a fresh-constructor call other than t's. Anything else (a parameter, a call result, a field) leaves the shape unrefined. The shape also needs !unknown_meta and none of blind_setmetatable, unseen_metatables, dynamic_code.\nWhy 3 suffices: a fresh constructor call yields a new table each execution, so the tables that reach a recognised rebinding site are the results of that site's own initialiser call, never the result of t's.\n\nWhere: types.rs records each setmetatable site on a shape (class, argument form: built, local, other) in metatable_builtin and computes fresh_class(F) and exact_class(VarId) after the last round; lower.rs method_call and which_call/folded_lookups take the exact class in place of ShapeInfo.classes when the receiver is a plain local with an exact class (method_targets and method_returns too). Tests: reference-pinned conformance cases for the idiom and for every way of defeating it (re-metatabling through an alias, a parameter, a closure, a method that calls setmetatable(self, ...), debug.setmetatable, getmetatable-driven rebinding).\n\nExpected effect on method_call: warm from 2.1x slower than LuaJIT to faster (elapsed about 1.6 ms against LuaJIT's 6.9 ms on CI scaling), wall about 25 ms lower on the NUC; cold still cannot reach 2x LuaJIT. Wins on no other row of the current 17.","files":null}]}